the king is driven into the center, and the other bishop can come back. This is lost.

01:14:17.200 --> 01:14:25.600
So that's the answer. One more thing. Note that after rook f8 takes here, we have bishop b6.

01:14:25.600 --> 01:14:32.240
Very important tactical detail that in this position, the rook on d7 is hanging. Otherwise,

01:14:32.240 --> 01:14:38.480
we would lose our bishop. Okay, so that's important. And after rook d3, we have a check,

01:14:39.360 --> 01:14:43.520
and now we can play bishop b6, and rook c8 is unstoppable checkmate.

01:14:43.520 --> 01:14:53.600
Okay, so what did we have in the game? Well, we had e6, bishop b6, bishop d6, bishop g3. I really

01:14:53.600 --> 01:14:58.880
like this move, because it trades on our own terms, and we open up the f-file. Same concept

01:14:58.880 --> 01:15:05.040
as the queen standoff on b3 and b6. Again, if you're a London player, this should be second

01:15:05.040 --> 01:15:08.800
nature to you, this bishop g3 kind of move. What do I mean by that if you're not a London player?

01:15:08.800 --> 01:15:15.040
Well, again, in the London, one of the most standard sequences is bishop d6, which is met

01:15:15.040 --> 01:15:19.520
with bishop g3. Now, of course, here, you're not trying to take with the f-pawn, you're trying to

01:15:19.520 --> 01:15:24.720
take toward the center and open up the file. But it's the same principle, just translated to a

01:15:24.720 --> 01:15:33.440
different file in the game, in the sense that we play f takes g3 in order to open up access to the

01:15:33.440 --> 01:15:41.040
f7 square. Then we chased the bishop away. There was one further detail. In response to bishop d1,

01:15:41.040 --> 01:15:46.880
we had a really sexy move. Okay, it's straightforward, but it's reliant on a

01:15:46.880 --> 01:15:52.880
really cool tactic. Who can spot this straightforward but nice move? It's not d6.

01:15:52.880 --> 01:15:59.680
I would say this is premature because of rook c6. And why have you—you've created an additional

01:15:59.680 --> 01:16:05.440
weakness. It's not rook d2. I don't want to move this rook away from the f-file.

01:16:05.440 --> 01:16:09.440
Can we do it with the other rook? That's a hint. Can you do it with the other rook?

01:16:10.320 --> 01:16:17.440
Turns out that you can. Rook to a1. And if you look carefully, you have now the move d6. That's

01:16:17.440 --> 01:16:23.520
it. Double attack on the rook and a discovered attack on the bishop. And, of course, black can

01:16:23.520 --> 01:16:27.840
continue playing here, but this is dead loss. Black is up a full exchange. White is up a full

01:16:27.840 --> 01:16:32.000
exchange. So here I would have played rook a1. This is what I was hoping would happen.

01:16:34.000 --> 01:16:39.120
Not taking no for an answer. Now we play rook a4. We lift the rook up. I don't think my technique

01:16:39.120 --> 01:16:43.360
was ideal. If you look at this with an engine, you'll probably find that I made some inaccuracies,

01:16:43.360 --> 01:16:48.400
but I think it's a moot point. We never let the wind slip. We're up two pawns, and now we just

01:16:48.400 --> 01:16:53.120
expand a little bit on the king side. Patient play g4. There's no hurry. Black has no plan.

01:16:53.120 --> 01:16:59.360
And now we decided to trade rooks. Note that after bishop d7, we would have forced a winning

01:16:59.360 --> 01:17:03.840
pawn endgame with rook to d8. This is actually a pretty important detail. You don't want a rook

01:17:03.840 --> 01:17:10.960
endgame because rook endgames tend to be very drawish. Rook d8 check in response to king c7.

01:17:11.840 --> 01:17:16.240
You should not play d6 check. I think this is a move which would be very tempting to some people,

01:17:17.120 --> 01:17:21.680
but after king takes eight, you've just given away your pawn. And white is probably still

01:17:21.680 --> 01:17:27.200
winning after bishop f5, but why? Why is this necessary? Just give a check. And now you can

01:17:27.200 --> 01:17:34.640
even go b4, cb, and c4, and this pawn decides the game. Or you can start with c4 and then play b4

01:17:34.640 --> 01:17:41.600
and c5. So plenty of ways to win. King e5, h5 is game over. And I really like bishop g8. I think

01:17:41.600 --> 01:17:47.600
this is the last flourish, avoiding blundering the bishop. And black just has no way to stop d6.

01:17:47.600 --> 01:17:53.440
The rook is literally just trapped here. That's it. d6, and our opponent resigned because the

01:17:53.440 --> 01:17:58.960
rook is trapped. So again, the final like 10-15 moves were in time pressure and should be taken

01:17:58.960 --> 01:18:02.880
with a grain of salt. I don't think my endgame technique was perfect, but I think it was

01:18:02.880 --> 01:18:08.880
sufficient to win the game. So that was complicated. That was very, a lot of meat on the bone there.

01:18:08.880 --> 01:18:13.840
Key takeaways from this game. What's the TLDR? First of all, in terms of the theory,

01:18:13.840 --> 01:18:20.400
queen a4 check is the best move against the delayed d5 variation. Our biggest mistake was to

01:18:20.400 --> 01:18:26.400
play, so d4 here is a very good move, but after knight e5, it's crucial to take back with the

01:18:26.400 --> 01:18:31.520
bishop, secure the bishop pair, and enjoy your life in that endgame. Remember that knight a3 is

01:18:31.520 --> 01:18:36.800
a very typical move in the alapin, and the knight b5 is not the only idea. You can drop the knight

01:18:36.800 --> 01:18:45.120
back to c2 and prepare d4. Then one of the critical moments, of course, d4, finding the

01:18:45.120 --> 01:18:51.120
opportunity to push the pawn, and bishop takes d5, being constantly on alert for tactics,

01:18:51.120 --> 01:18:56.560
even in positions where they seem very unlikely, and doing, you know, crossing your t's, dotting

01:18:56.560 --> 01:19:01.200
your i's, always considering captures, even if just for a single second. And once the pawn was

01:19:01.200 --> 01:19:07.680
won—oh, sorry, I keep doing that—once the pawn was won, I think the game-winning moves were d5

01:19:07.680 --> 01:19:12.800
and bishop f4, both moves aimed at prophylaxis, stopping your opponent's threats,

01:19:12.800 --> 01:19:17.600
positional and tactical. All right, guys, I think that concludes the presentation. That

